tasks.description:
Two products, written as product files under crates/spec-compiler/tests/fixtures/products/hot-reload/ (ticket-desk.jsonc is the model).

service-desk, versions v1 to v6:
- objects customer, asset and ticket. ticket references customer and asset, has a workflow on status, and a sla_due time field;
- queries and views: a ticket board showing customer.name through the reference, a customer list and an asset detail;
- forms, actions, live bindings and error messages on each screen;
- each version makes one kind of change on purpose:
  - v2: add a field and its form field and column;
  - v3: rename ticket.title to subject (adaptable), and customer.name to display_name read through the reference;
  - v4: move the board's route, add a screen, and change an error message's wording and a live binding's kinds;
  - v5: remove the asset screen, add a required operation input, and tighten one screen's auth to a role;
  - v6: multi-entity, changing 3 objects, 2 queries, 2 views and 5 ui elements at once.

knowledge-base, v1 only, installed beside it, for "the other product is untouched".

The coordinator compiles the bundles with specc bundle. A substrate test with a bless mode (STRATA_BLESS=1) installs the versions in order into one tenant, then writes each version's manifest and parts (strata_substrate::ui) to web/test/fixtures/hot-reload/<product>-v<n>/manifest.json and parts/<hash>.json.

A seeded generator of timelines, sharing one tenant:
- 2 to 8 tabs, held by 1 to 3 users, on screens of both products;
- upgrades along service-desk v1..v6, including rollbacks, plus removal and reinstall;
- operations that create and update records, and form drafts typed into open forms;
- disconnects and reconnects;
- a scheduler that delivers responses and notices in random order, with drops and delays.

Each timeline runs on the simulator with I1-I7 checked after every step and a final quiescence check (I3). A failing seed prints the seed and a minimal step list, shrunk by removing steps while it still fails. A committed seed set of at least 200 runs in the routine web tests. A larger sweep runs under an environment variable.

tests/e2e/hot-reload.spec.js runs against the dev stack, the way the existing Playwright projects start it. Several browser contexts sign in as the development logins: admin as owner, ines as editor, tomasz as author. The bundles are installed and upgraded through POST /api/products/install and /upgrade.

Headline scenarios:
- (1) title upgrade with 3 tabs over 2 users, and no reload (a window marker survives);
- (2) rename upgrade while a draft is being typed, and the draft survives;
- (3) multi-entity v6 upgrade while two users create tickets, after which every tab shows every ticket;
- (4) the screen is removed under an open tab;
- (5) auth tightened, with the member refused and the editor unaffected;
- (6) the knowledge-base tab is untouched by a service-desk upgrade, which the network log shows: no part requests.

User decision on 2026-11-21: hard trim and aggressive consolidation. 2,177 tests in 163 binaries; a cold run took about 50 minutes, and the test time was 1,936 s. Rules are in var/trim-brief.md on the stack/tests worktree:
- delete by default and keep concrete behaviour;
- keep at least one test per Verifies id;
- parse the spec at most once per binary;
- shrink or ignore scale tests.

Split into file groups by agent (backend 3 groups, kernel 2, spec-compiler, storage-sqlite+bff+substrate, the rest). Consolidating the binaries is WG-5354.

Found during the test trim (WG-5355). In crates/backend/tests, each start() seeds two tenants and hashes the nine development account passwords with Argon2 at production cost. Per-test startup dominates service.rs (36 s for 67 tests) and tokens.rs.

Options:
- tests start with a pre-hashed accounts file;
- a test-only lower Argon2 cost, behind the development feature and never reachable in production;
- one server shared per binary where tests do not interfere.

The production cost and the verification path must stay exactly as they are.

User decision on 2026-11-21: tests cover only what the specification states. Measured on stack/tests: 1,492 hand-written integration tests, of which 757 carry a Verifies line covering 537 requirement ids, and 735 trace to nothing.

The rule (var/spec-only-brief.md):
- delete every test without a Verifies line;
- keep one test per requirement id, at most two for distinct clauses;
- keep generated tests, the evolution catalogue, the Exegra and Lean differentials and the purity rule;
- in the spec compiler and spec-expr, keep a toolchain guard of about 40-60 tests: golden outputs, one refusal per layering rule, and the generated-code differentials.

Follows WG-5355 (first trim, about 5%).

User decision on 2026-11-21. badge-milestones is a test fixture product (crates/spec-compiler/tests/fixtures/products/badge-milestones.jsonc and its bundle), not a feature, but its badge vocabulary reads like one. Replace it with a fixture named after the primitives it exercises:
- a ledger unit;
- a trigger on ledger-posted with a threshold-crossing condition (using is_null for presence);
- an automation with a run key (once per person, SCH-1536 and D-6534);
- a created record;
- a notice and mail template;
- product install and upgrade.

The coverage and Verifies ids stay exactly as they are.

